    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S2532B\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to requiring chain\nrestaurants to label menu items that have a high content of sodium\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to increase consumer awareness of high sodi-\num content foods at chain restaurants across the state.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 adds a new section 1356 to the public health law to provide\nfor definitions to a \"chain restaurant\", \"daily value\", \"food service\nestablishment\", \"menu or menu board', and a \"standard menu item.\"\nFurther, provides industry guidance that within 1 year of the effective\ndate, the DOH shall publish guidance which explains the requirements of\nthis section, and how businesses can comply with them. The DOH shall\ndevelop an icon with accompanying text to be displayed adjacent to any\nmenu item which exceeds the higher than total daily recommended value\nfor sodium intake, no later than 1 year after the effective date. Begin-\nning 2 years after the effective date, chain restaurants in the state\nshall display the icon and text on their menus or menu boards. No later\nthan 6 years after the effective date, the DOH shall issue a report\nreviewing the impact of these provisions on menu item reformulation, as\nwell as consumer behavior.\n\nAny chain restaurant that violates these provisions shall be subject to\na penalty of no more than $250 per day for each location not in compli-\nance. A jurisdiction that has the same or substantially similar menu\nitem displays as required pursuant to a rule, regulation, ordinance,\nlocal law or policy issued by a jurisdiction shall be exempt from these\nprovisions.\n\nSection 2 provides for a severability clause.\n\nSection 3 provides for  the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nStudies have shown that the vast majority of dietary salt comes from\nprocessed foods and foods eaten outside of the home, such as food served\nat restaurants. While several companies have voluntarily made efforts to\nreduce salt in table foods, the U.S. Food and Drug Administration\ncontinues to press the food industry to reduce sodium content.  Consum-\ning too much sodium can increase risk of high blood pressure that can\nlead to heart attack and stroke. Only one in ten Americans meets the one\nteaspoon per day sodium guideline. High sodium intake accounts for the\ngreatest health problem related to food supply in the nation.  This bill\nseeks to reduce the daily sodium intake consumed by New Yorkers by\nrequiring the labeling of restaurant items which exceed the recommended\ndaily value for sedum as recommended by the FDA. With these new guide-\nlines, New Yorkers can make more informed choices about foods to order\nfor themselves and their families.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n2019-2020: S49030A/A3871A Ortiz\n\n2020-2021: S2532/No Same-As\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nEffective immediately.\n",
